  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This is a flat scarf worked from one short end to the other in "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "broken-rib-lace"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "broken rib lace"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", a four-row pattern that places two rib rows followed by an eyelet row and a purl row, knitted on 4 mm needles with DK yarn, finishing at 20 cm wide and 175 cm long after blocking. The rib column structure gives the fabric more visual depth than plain eyelet lace."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Flat construction suits this pattern because the rib rows require tracking the right side and wrong side: on RS rows work K1, P1 across the rib; on WS rows work the stitches as they appear (K the knits, P the purls). The eyelet row replaces the rib instruction with (YO, K2tog) across, so the columns appear broken at regular intervals. The scarf uses about 175 g (395 m / 432 yards) of DK yarn."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "heading": "Materials",
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "DK-weight wool or cotton yarn",
              "qty": "175 g (approx. 395 m / 432 yards)",
              "substitutions": "Any DK yarn. A smooth yarn shows the broken rib texture more clearly than a hairy one."
            },
            {
              "name": "4 mm needles (UK 8 / US 6)",
              "qty": "1 pair or 1 circular",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Blocking mat and rust-proof pins",
              "qty": "required",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Tapestry or darning needle",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Scissors",
              "qty": "1 pair",
              "substitutions": ""
            },
            {
              "name": "Measuring tape",
              "qty": "1",
              "substitutions": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "22 sts x 30 rows = 10 x 10 cm in stocking stitch on 4 mm needles. Broken rib lace opens to approximately 20 sts per 10 cm after blocking. The rib sections slightly compress the width; the eyelet rows open it again. Measure the gauge swatch after blocking."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitches used"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "K (knit), P (purl), "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "yarn-over"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "YO"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " (yarn over), "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "k2tog"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "K2tog"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pattern"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast on"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast on 44 stitches using the long-tail cast-on (a multiple of 2). Tail length: (12.6 x 44) / 10 + 15 = about 70 cm."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count check-in: 44 stitches after the cast-on."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Body"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 1 (RS): (K1, P1) to end. (44 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 2 (WS): (K1, P1) to end. (44 sts) [Work stitches as they appear: purl over knit, knit over purl.]"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 3 (RS eyelet): (YO, K2tog) to end. (44 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 4 (WS): P to end. (44 sts)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Repeat Rows 1-4 until the scarf measures 175 cm from the cast-on edge, blocked. Work to approximately 155 cm off the needles to allow for blocking expansion. At the halfway mark, count: still 44 stitches."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cast off"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Use a stretchy cast off worked in pattern on a Row 1 (rib RS row) to match the elasticity of the rib sections."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Common faults"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Rib columns out of alignment. After the eyelet row, the stitch positions shift slightly. On Row 1 after the eyelet, check that you are starting the rib with a K stitch over a purl bump (not a V). If the columns look offset, work one extra row of rib before starting the eyelet row next time."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitch count changing on the eyelet row. (YO, K2tog) must be worked in pairs across the full row. Count before and after every Row 3."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fabric too tight. Broken rib lace is slightly denser than plain eyelet. If the fabric feels stiff, go up half a millimetre in needle size."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in ends. Wet "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "blocking"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "blocking"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is required: soak, press out moisture, and pin flat to finished dimensions."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash in cool water and lay flat to dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The fern lace scarf adds directional increases and decreases to create a leaf-shaped motif. Or try a simple cable scarf for a completely different kind of texture."
          }
        ]
      }
